From d757d6abf413fb313e78ecc343ec8e52edb63876 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Abhinav Parihar <parihar@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 11:49:09 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] misc: fastrpc: fix invoke context leak on abrupt process exit

fastrpc_context_alloc() takes a reference on struct fastrpc_user via
fastrpc_user_get() for every invoke context it creates. If the owning
thread gives up on an invoke with -ETIMEDOUT, or the context is left
in fl->interrupted, that reference is never dropped unless the
context is later freed through fastrpc_context_put().

If the process exits abruptly before the DSP responds (or the
response never arrives), these leftover contexts stay parked on
fl->pending/fl->interrupted forever. Because each of them holds a
reference on fl, fl->refcount never reaches zero, so the unconditional
cleanup in fastrpc_user_free() -- which would otherwise free these
contexts along with any DMA maps they reference -- never runs. This
is a permanent kernel memory leak.

It also cannot be recovered later via SSR: fastrpc_device_release()
unconditionally removes fl from cctx->users, so a subsequent SSR's
fastrpc_notify_users() walk over cctx->users will never find this fl
again to reap its stuck contexts.

Fix this by having fastrpc_device_release() reclaim any leftover
fl->pending/fl->interrupted contexts itself, right after calling
fastrpc_release_current_dsp_process(). That call is expected to tear
down the DSP-side process counterpart, so anything still left on
these lists at this point is safe to release. If the DSP-side release
itself fails, that is a separate problem that needs its own
investigation, not a reason to keep leaking this process's contexts.

The leftover entries are moved to a local list with
list_splice_tail_init() under fl->lock and processed after dropping
the lock, since fastrpc_context_put() can recurse into
fastrpc_free_map() -> spin_lock(&map->fl->lock), which would deadlock
if called while fl->lock is still held.

Change-Id: Iff75870f1623061e7c990c8a25c037e65a153bbe
Signed-off-by: Abhinav Parihar <parihar@qti.qualcomm.com>
(cherry picked from commit 2042cbe5e6c2d68e57ce2541d47b6e6849ca3dab)
---
 drivers/misc/fastrpc.c | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
index 3a1dcbbb1823..1bb402b9622e 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
@@ -1788,10 +1788,28 @@ static int fastrpc_device_release(struct inode *inode, struct file *file)
 {
 	struct fastrpc_user *fl = (struct fastrpc_user *)file->private_data;
 	struct fastrpc_channel_ctx *cctx = fl->cctx;
+	struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx *ctx, *n;
+	struct list_head leftover;
 	unsigned long flags;
 
 	fastrpc_release_current_dsp_process(fl);
 
+	/*
+	 * fastrpc_release_current_dsp_process() is expected to tear down the
+	 * DSP-side process counterpart, so anything still in fl->pending or
+	 * fl->interrupted is safe to reclaim now.
+	 */
+	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&leftover);
+	spin_lock(&fl->lock);
+	list_splice_tail_init(&fl->pending, &leftover);
+	list_splice_tail_init(&fl->interrupted, &leftover);
+	spin_unlock(&fl->lock);
+
+	list_for_each_entry_safe(ctx, n, &leftover, node) {
+		list_del(&ctx->node);
+		fastrpc_context_put(ctx);
+	}
+
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&cctx->lock, flags);
 	list_del(&fl->user);
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&cctx->lock, flags);
-- 
